#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087877 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087452 :question/id #uuid "1e3c0253-226d-596c-a831-7c68ec76b21c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21741" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 2y = 2e^{-2x}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x) \\right) = I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = 2$ and $Q(x) = 2e^{-2x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = e^{2x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{2x}\\cdot y & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x)\\right){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{2x}) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c49e068-198f-59ab-8835-7018b02d7d0c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050608}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495375 :question-generator/id #uuid "3a975138-4eaf-5af2-b1a8-7230f629f92a" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186087877.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abc22f1d-811f-5bdc-b37d-0282e04